{"name":"Sleeping Giant","hex":"#786d5e","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sleeping giant","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Someone or something with great, latent strength."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sleeping_giant"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sleeping Giant (Connecticut)","blocks":[{"text":"Sleeping Giant, , is a rugged traprock mountain with a high point of 739 feet (225 m), located eight miles (13 km) north of New Haven, Connecticut. A prominent landscape feature visible for miles, the Sleeping Giant receives its name from its anthropomorphic resemblance to a slumbering human figure as seen from either the north or south. The Giant is known for its expansive clifftop vistas, rugged topography, and microclimate ecosystems. Most of the Giant is located within Sleeping Giant State Park. The mountain is a popular recreation site: over 30 miles (48 km) of hiking trails traverse it including 5 miles (8 km) of the 23-mile (37 km) Quinnipiac Trail. Quinnipiac University is located at Mount Carmel's foot in Hamden."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/SlGiant.jpg/330px-SlGiant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sleeping_Giant_(Connecticut)","wikidata":"Q3486619"},{"title":"Sleep","blocks":[{"text":"Sleep is a state of reduced mental and physical activity in which consciousness is altered and certain sensory activity is inhibited. During sleep, there is a marked decrease in muscle activity and interactions with the surrounding environment. While sleep differs from wakefulness in terms of the ability to react to stimuli, it still involves active brain patterns, making it more reactive than a coma or disorders of consciousness."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Domenico_Fetti_-_Sleeping_Girl_-_WGA7863.jpg/330px-Domenico_Fetti_-_Sleeping_Girl_-_WGA7863.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sleep","wikidata":"Q35831"},{"title":"Giant","blocks":[{"text":"In folklore, giants are beings of humanoid appearance, but are at times prodigious in size and strength or bear an otherwise notable appearance. The word giant is first attested in 1297 from Robert of Gloucester's chronicle. It is derived from the Gigantes of Greek mythology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Rhinegold_and_the_Valkyries_p_032.jpg/330px-Rhinegold_and_the_Valkyries_p_032.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giant","wikidata":"Q3707571"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/SlGiant.jpg/500px-SlGiant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/SlGiant.jpg/500px-SlGiant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASlGiant.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Hamden-CT-SleepingGiantMtn.jpg/500px-Hamden-CT-SleepingGiantMtn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Hamden-CT-SleepingGiantMtn.jpg/500px-Hamden-CT-SleepingGiantMtn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHamden-CT-SleepingGiantMtn.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Gianttower.jpg/500px-Gianttower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Gianttower.jpg/1280px-Gianttower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Gianttower.jpg/500px-Gianttower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGianttower.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Traprock_1.jpg/500px-Traprock_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Traprock_1.jpg/500px-Traprock_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Close-up of traprock on the Sleeping Giant","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATraprock_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Mill_River_with_Tornado_Damage_to_the_south%2C_Hamden%2C_CT_%2853227551329%29.jpg/500px-Mill_River_with_Tornado_Damage_to_the_south%2C_Hamden%2C_CT_%2853227551329%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Mill_River_with_Tornado_Damage_to_the_south%2C_Hamden%2C_CT_%2853227551329%29.jpg/1280px-Mill_River_with_Tornado_Damage_to_the_south%2C_Hamden%2C_CT_%2853227551329%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Mill_River_with_Tornado_Damage_to_the_south%2C_Hamden%2C_CT_%2853227551329%29.jpg/500px-Mill_River_with_Tornado_Damage_to_the_south%2C_Hamden%2C_CT_%2853227551329%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Damage around the southern part of the Blue Trail that follows the Mill River.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMill_River_with_Tornado_Damage_to_the_south%2C_Hamden%2C_CT_(53227551329).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Hanging_Hills.jpg/500px-Hanging_Hills.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"http